name: variables
boss: $VARIABLE
flavor: I hold what you've forgotten.
brief: A variable is name=value, with no spaces around the =. You read it back
brief: with $name. Command substitution $(cmd) runs a command and becomes its
brief: output, which is how you get a number out of wc and into a comparison.
brief:
brief: Quote your expansions. "$x" is one word even when the value has spaces;
brief: $x is however many words the value happens to split into. Nearly every
brief: shell script bug that survives review is a missing pair of quotes.
---
q: store the word @word@ in a variable called @var@
a: @var@=@word@
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: no spaces around the equals sign
---
q: %show% the value of @var@
a: @var@=@word@; echo $@var@
out: @word@
ref: echo
teach: echo
---
q: store the output of the date command in a variable called @tvar@
a: @tvar@=$(date)
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: $( ) runs a command and becomes its output
---
q: store the number of lines in @log@ in a variable called @cvar@
a: @cvar@=$(wc -l < @log@)
ref: wc
teach: wc -l
---
q: %show% the length of the value in @var@
a: @var@=@word@; echo ${#@var@}
out: [0-9]
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: a hash inside the braces gives the length
---
q: %show% @var@, or the word default if it is unset or empty
a: echo ${@var@:-default}
out: default
ref: sh
teach: sh
---
q: %show% the line count of @log@ inside a sentence reading "lines: N"
a: echo "lines: $(wc -l < @log@)"
out: ^lines: [0-9]+$
ref: echo
teach: echo
---
q: export @mvar@ with the value @word@ so child processes inherit it
a: export @mvar@=@word@
ref: export
teach: export
---
q: %show% every exported variable in the environment
a: env
out: =
ref: env
teach: env
---
q: run one command with @mvar@ set to @word@, without changing your own shell
a: env @mvar@=@word@ sh -c 'echo $@mvar@'
out: @word@
ref: env
teach: env
---
q: run a command with the environment completely emptied first
a: env -i sh -c 'echo empty'
out: empty
ref: env
teach: env
---
q: run a command with @mvar@ removed from the environment
a: env -u @mvar@ sh -c 'echo gone'
out: gone
ref: env
teach: env -u
---
q: %show% the environment with entries separated by NUL rather than newline
a: env -0
out: =
ref: env
teach: env -0
---
q: remove the variable @var@ from your shell entirely
a: unset @var@
ref: unset
teach: unset
---
q: read a line from standard input into a variable called @var@
a: read @var@
ref: read
teach: read
---
q: read a line into @var@ without letting backslashes escape anything
a: read -r @var@
ref: read
teach: read -r
hint: -r is what you want essentially always; without it a backslash vanishes
---
q: prompt with "name: " and read the reply into @var@
a: read -p "name: " @var@
ref: read
teach: read -p
---
q: %show% the first field of @csv@, using a variable to hold the separator
a: d=,; cut -d"$d" -f1 @csv@
out: ^gateway$
ref: cut
teach: cut -d
---
q: run a single command in a child shell using the -c flag
a: sh -c 'echo @word@'
out: ^@word@$
ref: sh
teach: sh -c
---
q: remove every alias at once
a: unalias -a
ref: unalias
teach: unalias -a
---
q: describe what ls is, in a full sentence rather than just a path
a: command -V ls
out: ls
ref: command
teach: command -V
---
q: %find% ls using a safe default PATH rather than whatever yours happens to be
a: command -p ls @txt@
out: @txt@
ref: command
teach: command -p
---
q: %show% every place in your PATH that has a command called sh
a: which -a sh
out: sh
ref: which
teach: which -a
